  % abstract = {Epistemic rationality concerns multiple aspects of human reasoning. It involves not only forming appropriate doxastic attitudes and representing uncertainty, but also managing unresolved uncertainty. These activities reflect different dimensions of epistemic agency. Accordingly, understanding epistemic rationality requires examining not only how agents represent the world, but also how they manage uncertainty through the formation, evaluation, and revision of their epistemic attitudes.
  
This thesis develops a systematic decision-theoretic framework for understanding three central aspects of epistemic rationality within their relevant contexts: how agents should respond to unresolved uncertainty, how they should represent uncertainty through rational credences, and how they should decide whether to pursue further inquiry.

First, the thesis develops a decision-theoretic account of suspension of judgment. It argues that suspension can be represented within a credal framework while preserving the distinction between belief, disbelief, and suspension. Specifically, an agent should suspend when delaying commitment has greater expected utility than immediately committing to either belief or disbelief. This shows how rational suspension can be understood as a rational choice within a decision-theoretic framework. It extends a Lockean-style relationship between credence and all-out doxastic attitudes by accommodating suspension alongside belief and disbelief through context-sensitive thresholds.

Building on the decision-theoretic analysis of doxastic attitudes, the thesis then examines how agents should form rational credences given uncertainty. It develops an account of moral encroachment on credences by emphasizing the role of priors in credence formation. The account argues that moral considerations can influence the rationality of credences while preserving core Bayesian commitments. This is achieved by allowing relevant moral and epistemic commitments to constrain prior selection rather than altering the evidential role of information.

Finally, the thesis extends this decision-theoretic perspective from how agents represent uncertainty to how they act on it. It develops a two-threshold model for the epistemic norms of inquiry based on expected epistemic utility. The framework systematically explains when inquiry is rationally permissible, prohibited, required, or supererogatory, and how these different normative statuses arise and relate to one another. In particular, it provides an account of supererogatory inquiry, a category that has often been overlooked in existing discussions. It explains how some inquiries can have sufficiently high expected epistemic utility to merit epistemic praise without being required.
 
Together, this thesis develops a decision-theoretic perspective on epistemic agency under uncertainty. It shows how decision-theoretic reasoning can evaluate epistemic attitudes and activities while preserving the central roles of evidence and probabilistic reasoning. More broadly, it demonstrates how epistemic rationality concerns not only representing uncertainty but also managing it through attitudes, credences, and inquiries.},
